# Day 1 — Introduction to RISC-V

## Overview

Day 1 introduced the basic concepts required to begin working with the RISC-V architecture and the MYTH workshop environment.

The session focused on understanding the RISC-V instruction set, the software tools used during the workshop, and the basic flow used to compile and execute RISC-V programs.

## Topics Covered

### 1. RISC-V Architecture

RISC-V is an open instruction set architecture based on the RISC design philosophy.

The architecture provides a simple and modular instruction set that can be used for both learning and practical processor development.

### 2. RISC-V Instructions

The session introduced the structure and purpose of RISC-V instructions.

Understanding instructions at the assembly level helps in connecting software operations with the underlying processor hardware.

### 3. Registers

RISC-V provides a set of general-purpose registers that are used to hold data and intermediate results while instructions are executed.

Learning the role of these registers is important for understanding assembly programs and processor operation.

### 4. Tool Flow

The workshop environment provides tools that allow RISC-V programs to be written, compiled, and executed.

The basic flow can be represented as:

```text
Source Program
      ↓
RISC-V Compiler
      ↓
Assembly / Machine Code
      ↓
Execution
      ↓
Output
```

## Practical Work

During this session, I followed the workshop instructions and performed the introductory exercises using the provided RISC-V environment.

The screenshots in this folder document the steps and outputs obtained during the session.

## Key Takeaways

* Understood the purpose of the RISC-V ISA.
* Became familiar with the basic RISC-V workflow.
* Learned the importance of registers in instruction execution.
* Started working with the RISC-V software environment.
* Connected assembly-level operations with processor execution.

